STAGE-1 EXPEDIENT (declaration-only): drop `cmd:groff` from gettext's
BUILD_PREREQUIRES.

This is the cut that broke the netpbm/cmake deadlock. It is the cheapest edge in
the whole knot: one line, in one recipe, and it releases `gettext` ->
`cmd:autopoint` -> `xz_utils` -> `devel:liblzma` -> `zstd` -> `openssl3`.

THE PACKAGE ITSELF IS NOT CRIPPLED. Unlike the autoconf cut, nothing was removed
from the build or the install: groff is simply never *executed*, so the shipped
package contains the real, complete HTML man pages. What is untrue after this
patch is only the recipe's declared prerequisite set. That is why this is filed
as stage-1 bookkeeping rather than a functional regression -- but it is still
recorded, because a *rebuild from modified man page sources* would silently lose
the regeneration step (see "the one real risk" below).

## Why groff was in there

Its only appearance in gettext-1.0 is as a man-page-to-HTML converter, in exactly
two files (evidence from the 32 MB tarball, unpacked and grepped):

    gettext-1.0/gettext-tools/man/Makefile.in:3957:   MAN2HTML = groff -mandoc -Thtml
    gettext-1.0/gettext-runtime/man/Makefile.in:2299: MAN2HTML = groff -mandoc -Thtml

and nowhere else -- `grep -rn groff` over every Makefile.in / Makefile.am /
configure / configure.ac in the tree returns those two definitions, their two
surrounding comments, and nothing more. groff is not used for typesetting, PDF,
PostScript, or any part of the compile.

## Why the build does not need it, stated correctly

An earlier version of this note claimed `all-am` "never depends on $(man_HTML)".
**That is wrong**, and the wrong reason is worth recording because it would make
a reader trust the cut for the wrong cause. The dependency chain really does
reach the HTML files (gettext-tools/man/Makefile.in):

    4096:  all-am: Makefile $(MANS) all-local
    4225:  all-local: html-local
    4285:  html-local: $(man_HTML)

So `make all` *does* ask for `msgcmp.1.html` and its 20 siblings. The reason
groff is never invoked is different, and is stated by the tarball itself at
Makefile.in:4220:

    # We distribute both the man pages and their HTML equivalent.

All of them ship prebuilt -- 21 files in `gettext-tools/man`, 6 in
`gettext-runtime/man` -- and each `$(man_HTML)` entry has a single explicit
prerequisite, its own man page (`msgcmp.1.html: msgcmp.1`, Makefile.in:4294 ff).
make only rebuilds a target whose prerequisite is *strictly newer*, and a scan of
the pristine tarball finds **no** `.1` newer than its `.1.html`. Every one of
those rules is therefore already satisfied on arrival and its recipe is never
run.

The same argument covers `cmd:makeinfo`, which was left in place: gettext-tools/
doc does put `$(INFO_DEPS)` in `all-am` (Makefile.in:4318, INFO_DEPS =
$(srcdir)/gettext.info), but `gettext.info` ships prebuilt and dated 2026-01-28
against `gettext.texi` at 2026-01-27 -- already newer, so the rule at
Makefile.in:4150 does not fire. `html-split` likewise resolves to the shipped
`gettext_toc.html` and its 36 siblings, and uses `TEXI2HTML = @PERL@
build-aux/texi2html` rather than makeinfo anyway. This matters because the image's
`makeinfo` is the non-functional `texinfo_bootstrap` stub that kills any port
which actually invokes it (see autoconf-2.72-doc-cut-stage1.patch) -- gettext
gets away with it purely because it never calls it.

Confirmed empirically, not just by reading: the successful build log
(`/boot/home/c-gettext.log`, 23405 lines, kept at
`/opt/haiku/logs/agent-cycle/c-gettext.log`) contains no groff invocation and no
makeinfo invocation, and ends with all five hpkgs created.

## The one real risk this leaves behind

If anyone ever patches a gettext man page, the corresponding `.1` becomes newer
than the shipped `.1.html`, the rule fires, and the build dies on a missing
`groff` -- or, worse, if a fake groff is ever put on PATH, installs a garbage
HTML page. So the failure mode is loud (missing command) unless someone stubs it.
Do not stub it.

## Why not the alternatives

- **Build a stage-1 `groff` instead.** Much more expensive and it does not even
  avoid this file. groff's own BUILD_PREREQUIRES needs *five* unavailable
  commands -- `cmd:pnmcrop`, `cmd:pnmtopng`, `cmd:pnmtops` (netpbm) and
  `cmd:psselect` (psutils) -- plus `cmd:makeinfo`, which is the broken stub. So
  that route means cutting five edges across two packages instead of one edge
  across one, and netpbm is itself gated on libjasper/libtiff/libpng16/libxml2.
- **Timestamp-flattening (the libtool trick).** Would also work, and is strictly
  more robust -- it would survive the man-page-edit case by disabling the rule
  rather than relying on it being satisfied. It was not needed here because the
  tarball's own timestamps are already consistent. Reach for it if the risk above
  ever materialises.

Applies to: input-source-packages/develop/sources/gettext-1.0-1/gettext-1.0.recipe

--- a/gettext-1.0.recipe
+++ b/gettext-1.0.recipe
@@ BUILD_PREREQUIRES
+# STAGE-1 NOTE (graviton): cmd:groff removed from BUILD_PREREQUIRES.
+# groff appears in this tarball only as MAN2HTML in gettext-{runtime,tools}/man/
+# Makefile.in (:2299 / :3957), i.e. man-page-to-HTML conversion.
+# "all-am: Makefile $(MANS) all-local" -> "all-local: html-local" ->
+# "html-local: $(man_HTML)" does reach the html files, but the tarball ships all
+# 27 of them prebuilt ("We distribute both the man pages and their HTML
+# equivalent", Makefile.in:4220) and no .1 is newer than its .1.html, so every
+# rule is already satisfied and groff is never executed. Verified: no groff in
+# the build log. Restore this line once a real groff exists -- it is behind the
+# netpbm/psutils cycle. If a gettext man page is ever patched, the rule WILL
+# fire and the build will fail loudly on missing groff: flatten the source
+# timestamps then (see libtool-2.5.4-no-bootstrap.patch), do not stub groff.
 BUILD_PREREQUIRES="
 	cmd:aclocal
 	cmd:autoconf
 	cmd:automake
 	cmd:autoheader
 	cmd:gcc$secondaryArchSuffix
-	cmd:groff
 	cmd:gzip
 	cmd:ld$secondaryArchSuffix
 	cmd:libtoolize$secondaryArchSuffix
 	cmd:make
 	cmd:makeinfo
 	"
